# Genus invariance under algebraically closed constant field extension

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.genusFF_eq_of_constantFieldExtension_of_isAlgClosed`
(`729c32da-a2e4-5d19-9e97-4cb53850663c`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `de1dccfb-35d0-5415-9e5e-71b0e5fa33ed`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$, $F$, $K'$, $F'$ be fields with algebra maps $K \to F$, $K' \to F'$, $K \to K'$, $F \to F'$
and $K \to F'$, the last compatible with the towers $K \to K' \to F'$ and $K \to F \to F'$, and
suppose $K$ and $K'$ are algebraically closed. Assume $F$ is a function field of one variable over
$K$ in the sense that some $x \in F$ is transcendental over $K$ and $F$ is finite-dimensional over
the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$, and likewise that some $x \in F'$ is transcendental
over $K'$ with $F'$ finite-dimensional over $K'(x)$. Assume further that $F/K$ and $F'/K'$ both
satisfy `IsCurveOver`: every nonzero element has an associated divisor recording its orders at all
places and of degree $0$, each place having residue field finite over the base field, and the module
of Kähler differentials of the function field over the base field free of rank one. Here a place of
$F/K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself, and a
principal ideal ring. Finally, assume the $K'$-intermediate field of $F'$ generated by the image of
$F$ is all of $F'$. Then the two adelic genera agree: $\dim_{K'} H^1(0) = \dim_K H^1(0)$, that is,
`genusFF K' F' = genusFF K F`, where the genus is the dimension over the base field of the
repartition cohomology group $H^1$ of the zero divisor.

This is the invariance of the genus of an algebraic function field of one variable under extension
of an algebraically closed constant field, in arbitrary characteristic, formulated for the adelic
(repartition) definition of the genus. It is used throughout the curve-theoretic part of the
development, for instance in comparing orders of divisors after a constant field extension and in
the Abel–Jacobi and point-counting statements over finite fields.

